# \(X_\pi\)-pulse generation

This directory contains the two pulses used by 1qb-DB:

- Gaussian \(X_\pi\): baseline;
- resonant composite \(X_\pi\): robust candidate.

The experiment supports resonant control only, so the selected pulse has
\(\Delta(t)=0\). A common amplitude error is applied to both quadratures:

$$
H_c(t)=\frac{\Omega_x(t)X+\Omega_y(t)Y}{2},\qquad
(\Omega_x,\Omega_y)\rightarrow(1+\epsilon)(\Omega_x,\Omega_y).
$$

## Robust candidate

The earlier resonant BARQ result is not sufficiently flat over 60 cycles, and
the stronger TTC result requires unsupported detuning. Therefore, the current
candidate was reoptimized directly for the experimental target:

- error grid: \(-3\%\) to \(+3\%\);
- DB range: \(0\) to \(60\) cycles;
- four resonant segments with rotation angles
  \((\pi,\pi,2\pi,\pi)\);
- optimized phases
  \((0,1.822673,5.470164,1.822673)\).

The cost is

$$
\max_\epsilon[1-\min_{0\le n\le60}P_0(n,\epsilon)]
+10\max_\epsilon[1-F_{\rm gate}(\epsilon)].
$$

The first term directly suppresses the DB oscillation. The fidelity term
prevents selecting a pulse that looks flat but no longer implements \(X_\pi\).

## Validation

| pulse | detuning | peak \(T_g\Omega\) | \(P_0(60)\), \(-3\%/+3\%\) | \(\Delta P_0\), \(-3\%/+3\%\) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Gaussian | \(0\) | \(7.00\) | \(0.6545/0.6545\) | \(1.000/1.000\) |
| resonant composite | \(0\) | \(31.42\) | \(0.999996/0.999996\) | \(3.85\times10^{-6}/3.85\times10^{-6}\) |
| earlier resonant BARQ | \(0\) | \(22.00\) | \(0.9782/0.8835\) | \(0.0218/0.1165\) |

Here \(\Delta P_0=\max P_0-\min P_0\) over cycles \(0\ldots60\). The composite
pulse uses total rotation area \(5\pi\), so it needs a longer gate or a higher
peak drive than the Gaussian.

## Reuse

Directly usable files are in [`waveforms/`](waveforms/):

- `gaussian_x_pi.csv`;
- `resonant_composite_robust_x_pi.csv`;
- `resonant_composite_robust_params.json`;
- `metadata.json`, including settings, validation, versions, and checksums.

The CSV columns are:

```text
time_over_Tg,Tg_omega_x,Tg_omega_y,Tg_omega_envelope,phase_rad,Tg_delta
```

Regenerate the files from the saved parameters:

```bash
conda run -n curve python \
  proposals/1qb-DB/X_gate_pulse_generation/generate_waveforms.py
```

Add `--reoptimize` only when intentionally searching for new composite phases.
The older BARQ/TTC files remain for comparison and are not selected for this
experiment.

## AWG-rate export

The files above use a *normalized* time column, `time_over_Tg` in \([0,1]\), on
a 2501-point grid. That is an integration and plotting resolution, not a sample
rate, and it carries no information about the gate duration. For an AWG the
pulse must be resampled onto the instrument clock:

```bash
conda run -n curve python \
  proposals/1qb-DB/X_gate_pulse_generation/export_coarse_waveform.py
```

The default matches the instrument in use, \(T_g=100\) ns at 1 GSa/s, and
writes [`waveforms/resonant_composite_robust_x_pi_100samples_1GSa.csv`](waveforms/resonant_composite_robust_x_pi_100samples_1GSa.csv)
with columns

```text
sample_index,t_ns,I_norm,Q_norm,rabi_x_MHz,rabi_y_MHz,rabi_envelope_MHz,phase_rad
```

`I_norm`/`Q_norm` are scaled so the envelope peaks at 1.0; the `*_MHz` columns
are the same waveform expressed as an ordinary Rabi frequency
\(\Omega/2\pi\). Feed I and Q to the two mixer quadratures; there is no
detuning and no phase to program separately, since the segment phases are
already folded into I/Q. Peak drive is \(\Omega/2\pi=49.92\) MHz, a consequence
of the \(5\pi\) total rotation area — at \(T_g=100\) ns this pulse needs
\(4.5\times\) the Gaussian peak amplitude. `--gate-ns` trades that peak against
duration.

### Why 100 samples loses nothing

The candidate is analytic: four `sin^2` segments of 20/20/40/20 ns with
constant phase within each segment. Within a segment the Hamiltonians at
different times commute, so the segment unitary depends only on the
accumulated area \(\int\Omega\,dt\); and a uniform sum of `sin^2` over a whole
number \(N>1\) of samples reproduces that area exactly, for any sampling
offset, because \(\sum_n\cos(2\pi(n+a)/N)=0\). Both conditions hold on the 1 ns
grid, so the zero-order-hold staircase implements the ideal composite unitary
to machine precision, at every amplitude error.

`verify_coarse_waveform.py` checks this end to end and prints:

| quantity | 100 samples @ 1 GSa/s | analytic composite |
|---|---:|---:|
| total rotation area | \(5\pi\) to \(4\times10^{-16}\) rad | \(5\pi\) |
| \(\lVert U-U_{\rm exact}\rVert_F\) | \(9.8\times10^{-16}\) | — |
| \(1-F\) at \(\epsilon=\pm3\%\) | \(1.784\times10^{-10}\) | \(1.784\times10^{-10}\) |
| \(\Delta P_0\) over 60 cycles, \(\epsilon=\pm3\%\) | \(3.853\times10^{-6}\) | \(3.853\times10^{-6}\) |

The margin is large: the export stays exact for every sample count
\(N\geq10\) that is a multiple of 5. It fails only at \(N=5\), where the
shortest segment collapses to a single sample and the area identity no longer
applies.
